// Rep Max questions: Business (Pearson Edexcel 1BS0), GCSE. One question per line, no commas between lines.
// Fields: id (unique), s (subject), t (topic id from spec.json), lv (1 warm-up, 2 working set, 3 hardest; Higher-only topics are 3),
// q (question; maths between $ signs, backslashes doubled), code (optional), o (four options), a (index 0-3 of the right one), x (explanation).
// Written by AI (Sonnet) and checked independently by AI (Opus), 15 Nov 2026; logs in the library's provenance folder.
{"id":"gbu-001","s":"business","t":"1.1.1","lv":1,"q":"A baker starts selling vegan cakes because more customers now want plant-based food. What has caused this new business idea?","o":["A fall in interest rates","A new law on pricing","A merger with a rival","A change in customer tastes"],"a":3,"x":"New ideas often come from changing customer wants. Here, rising demand for plant-based food gave the baker an opportunity.","w":["Interest rates change borrowing costs, not what food customers want.","The question mentions no law; the cause is customers wanting plant-based food.","A merger joins firms together and doesn't explain a shift in what customers want.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-002","s":"business","t":"1.1.2","lv":2,"q":"Zara puts £10,000 of her own savings into a new shop. The shop fails after a year. Which risk does this show?","o":["Increased profit from lower costs","Business failure and loss of money invested","Gaining independence at work","Reward for taking a chance"],"a":1,"x":"Business failure is a key risk of enterprise. Entrepreneurs who invest their own money can lose it if the business fails.","w":["Lower costs and profit would be a reward, but Zara's shop failed and lost her money.","","Independence is a benefit of enterprise, not a risk.","Reward is the opposite of risk: Zara lost her savings."]}
{"id":"gbu-003","s":"business","t":"1.1.3","lv":2,"q":"A café buys ingredients costing £2 for each sandwich and sells it for £5. What is the value added per sandwich?","o":["£3","£2","£5","£7"],"a":0,"x":"Added value is selling price minus the cost of bought-in inputs: £5 - £2 = £3.","w":["","£2 is the input cost, not the value added.","£5 is the selling price; you must subtract the £2 of ingredients.","Added cost to price instead of subtracting."]}
{"id":"gbu-004","s":"business","t":"1.2.1","lv":1,"q":"A customer chooses a shop because it is close to home and open late. Which customer need is this?","o":["Quality","Choice","Convenience","Price"],"a":2,"x":"Convenience means making buying easy, for example by being nearby or open at useful times.","w":["Quality is about how good the product is, not location or opening hours.","Choice is about the range on offer, not being close and open late.","","Price is about cost; the question talks about location and hours."]}
{"id":"gbu-005","s":"business","t":"1.2.2","lv":1,"q":"Which is an example of primary market research?","o":["Government population statistics","A questionnaire given to local customers","A report bought from a research firm","Articles found on the internet"],"a":1,"x":"Primary research is new data collected first-hand, such as surveys or interviews. The other options use existing secondary data.","w":["Government statistics are secondary data, collected by someone else.","","A bought report is secondary research, not collected first-hand.","Internet articles are secondary; others gathered that data."]}
{"id":"gbu-006","s":"business","t":"1.2.3","lv":1,"q":"Which is an example of segmenting a market by age?","o":["Selling gym membership to people on high incomes","Selling coats to people who live in cold areas","Selling toys for 3 to 5 year olds","Selling football kits to fans"],"a":2,"x":"Segmentation by age splits customers into age groups. The other options use income, location or lifestyle.","w":["That segments by income, not age.","That segments by location, not age.","","Football fans is a lifestyle or interest segment, not an age group."]}
{"id":"gbu-007","s":"business","t":"1.2.3","lv":2,"q":"A market map of trainers shows no brands at high price and high quality. What might this gap suggest to a new business?","o":["It proves no customers would ever buy there","The market is already full","It no longer needs any market research","There may be an opportunity to sell a product there"],"a":3,"x":"A gap on a market map may show an opportunity. It does not prove there is demand, so research is still needed to check enough customers would buy.","w":["A gap doesn't prove nobody would buy; it may show an opportunity.","A gap means that part of the market is empty, not full.","Research is still needed to check that enough customers would buy.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-008","s":"business","t":"1.2.4","lv":2,"q":"A new florist opens next to a supermarket that sells cheap flowers. Which choice is most likely to help the florist compete?","o":["Offer better quality and expert, personal service","Sell the same basic bunches as the supermarket","Stock a smaller range of flowers","Open for fewer hours each week"],"a":0,"x":"A small florist is unlikely to beat a supermarket on price. It competes where the supermarket is weaker: quality, product range and customer service.","w":["","Selling the same basic bunches means a small shop can't win on price.","A smaller range removes a way to stand out from the supermarket.","Fewer opening hours makes the florist less convenient for customers."]}
{"id":"gbu-009","s":"business","t":"1.3.1","lv":1,"q":"Which is a non-financial objective of a new business?","o":["Making a profit of £20,000","Gaining independence","Raising sales by 10%","Winning 5% of the market"],"a":1,"x":"Independence is a non-financial aim. Profit, sales and market share targets are financial objectives.","w":["A £20,000 profit is a financial objective.","","Sales growth is a financial objective.","Market share is a financial objective too."]}
{"id":"gbu-010","s":"business","t":"1.3.2","lv":1,"q":"A stall sells 240 scarves at £15 each. What is the revenue?","o":["£3,600","£255","£360","£2,400"],"a":0,"x":"Revenue = price x quantity sold = £15 x 240 = £3,600.","w":["","Added the price and quantity instead of multiplying.","Place-value slip: that is $15 \\times 24$, not $15 \\times 240$.","Multiplied 240 by 10 instead of by 15."]}
{"id":"gbu-011","s":"business","t":"1.3.2","lv":2,"q":"Fixed costs are £6,000. Each unit sells for £5 and has variable costs of £2. What is the break-even output?","o":["1,200 units","3,000 units","857 units","2,000 units"],"a":3,"x":"Break-even = fixed costs / (price - variable cost per unit) = 6,000 / 3 = 2,000. Using the price alone gives 1,200.","w":["Divided by the price alone and forgot to subtract variable cost.","Divided by the variable cost of £2 instead of the £3 contribution.","Added price and variable cost ($5 + 2$) instead of subtracting.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-012","s":"business","t":"1.3.2","lv":2,"q":"A business breaks even at 400 units a month. It sells 650 units. What is its margin of safety?","o":["400 units","1,050 units","250 units","150 units"],"a":2,"x":"Margin of safety = actual sales - break-even output = 650 - 400 = 250 units.","w":["That is the break-even output, not the margin of safety.","Added sales and break-even instead of subtracting.","","Subtraction slip: $650 - 400 = 250$, not 150."]}
{"id":"gbu-013","s":"business","t":"1.3.2","lv":3,"q":"Fixed costs are £9,000. Price is £12 and variable cost is £7 per unit. The business sells 2,400 units. What is its profit?","o":["£12,000","£28,800","£2,400","£3,000"],"a":3,"x":"Profit per unit before fixed costs is £5. 2,400 x £5 = £12,000, then take off £9,000 = £3,000. £12,000 forgets fixed costs.","w":["Forgot to take the £9,000 fixed costs off the £12,000.","Found revenue (2,400 × £12) and ignored all the costs.","Just reused the number of units sold; it isn't a profit calculation.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-014","s":"business","t":"1.3.3","lv":1,"q":"Why can a profitable business still run out of cash?","o":["Profit and cash are always the same","Profit is paid out as tax on the day it is earned","Customers may pay late while bills must be paid now","Cash only matters to large businesses"],"a":2,"x":"Profit is not the same as cash. Timing matters: if money comes in later than payments go out, a business can run short of cash.","w":["Profit and cash are different; timing can leave a firm short of cash.","Tax isn't paid on the day profit is earned, and that isn't the issue here.","","Cash flow matters to every business, large or small."]}
{"id":"gbu-015","s":"business","t":"1.3.3","lv":3,"q":"January: opening £1,000, inflows £4,000, outflows £3,200. February: inflows £3,500, outflows £4,300. What is February's closing balance?","o":["£1,000","-£800","£1,800","£800"],"a":0,"x":"January closing = 1,000 + 4,000 - 3,200 = £1,800. That is February's opening. Then 1,800 + 3,500 - 4,300 = £1,000.","w":["","Used only February's net flow (3,500 − 4,300) and forgot the £1,800 opening balance.","That's February's opening balance; you stopped before adding February's flows.","Took inflows from outflows the wrong way round and ignored the opening balance."]}
{"id":"gbu-016","s":"business","t":"1.3.4","lv":2,"q":"A small shop must pay a supplier now, but its own customers will not pay it for two weeks. Which source of finance best covers this short gap?","o":["A five-year bank loan","A bank overdraft","Share capital from new owners","Venture capital"],"a":1,"x":"An overdraft is a short-term source of finance for brief cash gaps. Loans, share capital and venture capital are long-term sources, used for bigger needs over longer periods.","w":["A five-year loan is too long and costly for a two-week gap.","","Share capital is long-term and gives away ownership; not for a short gap.","Venture capital funds long-term growth, not a two-week gap."]}
{"id":"gbu-017","s":"business","t":"1.4.1","lv":2,"q":"Which business type has unlimited liability and one owner?","o":["Private limited company","Public limited company","Sole trader","Partnership"],"a":2,"x":"A sole trader has one owner who is personally liable for the business's debts. A partnership also has unlimited liability but two or more owners. Companies have limited liability.","w":["Private limited companies have limited liability, not unlimited.","Plcs have limited liability and many shareholders.","","Partnerships have unlimited liability but two or more owners, not one."]}
{"id":"gbu-018","s":"business","t":"1.4.2","lv":2,"q":"A bakery wants to move to a place where it will get lots of passing customers. Which location factor is this?","o":["Proximity to the market","Proximity to raw materials","Availability of labour","Costs of premises"],"a":0,"x":"Being near customers matters for shops that rely on footfall. Other factors include materials, workers and costs.","w":["","Raw materials matter to manufacturers, not a shop wanting passing trade.","Labour supply is about finding staff, not attracting customers.","Premises costs are about rent and expense, not footfall."]}
{"id":"gbu-019","s":"business","t":"1.4.3","lv":1,"q":"Which is part of the marketing mix?","o":["Profit","Promotion","Production","People management"],"a":1,"x":"The four Ps are product, price, promotion and place.","w":["Profit is a business outcome, not one of the four Ps.","","Production is an operations activity, not one of the four Ps.","People management is HR, not part of the marketing mix."]}
{"id":"gbu-020","s":"business","t":"1.4.4","lv":2,"q":"Why does a bank often ask for a business plan before lending money?","o":["It proves the business will definitely make a profit","It removes all risk that the loan will not be repaid","It replaces the need for the owner to do market research","It shows plans and forecasts so the bank can judge the risk"],"a":3,"x":"A business plan sets out aims, market research, forecasts and finance needs. Forecasts are not guarantees, but they help a lender judge risk, and planning helps the owner reduce risk.","w":["Forecasts are only estimates; they can't prove a profit.","Risk can't be removed completely; a plan only helps judge it.","The plan is built on market research; it doesn't replace it.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-021","s":"business","t":"1.5.1","lv":2,"q":"Shareholders want higher dividends but employees want higher wages. What does this show?","o":["A shortage of cash","A rise in revenue","A conflict between stakeholders","A change in the law"],"a":2,"x":"Stakeholders have different aims. Paying more to one group can reduce what is available for another.","w":["Nothing says cash is short; the issue is two groups wanting different things.","Nothing here says revenue is rising; the issue is competing demands.","","No law is changing; the groups simply have different aims."]}
{"id":"gbu-022","s":"business","t":"1.5.2","lv":1,"q":"What is e-commerce?","o":["Selling only in high-street shops","Printing leaflets for customers","Making goods using machines","Buying and selling goods and services online"],"a":3,"x":"E-commerce means trading over the internet, for example through a website or app.","w":["That's traditional high-street retail, not online trading.","Leaflets are print marketing, not trading online.","That's production, not buying and selling online.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-023","s":"business","t":"1.5.3","lv":2,"q":"A shop sells a toaster that stops working after two days. Under consumer law, what must goods be?","o":["Of satisfactory quality and fit for purpose","Cheaper than rival goods","Made in the UK","Sold only by large firms"],"a":0,"x":"The Consumer Rights Act says goods must be of satisfactory quality, fit for purpose and as described. Faulty goods can be returned.","w":["","Consumer law doesn't require goods to be cheaper than rivals.","No law says goods must be made in the UK.","Consumer law covers every seller, large or small."]}
{"id":"gbu-024","s":"business","t":"1.5.4","lv":3,"q":"The pound becomes stronger against the euro. A UK firm exports to Europe and keeps its prices in pounds the same. What is the likely effect?","o":["Its goods become cheaper for European buyers","Its goods become more expensive for European buyers","Its UK costs fall by the same amount","Its profits always rise"],"a":1,"x":"A stronger pound means euro buyers need more euros to pay the same £ price, so exports can become less competitive.","w":["Reversed: a stronger pound makes exports dearer for euro buyers, not cheaper.","","UK costs are in pounds, so they don't fall because of the exchange rate.","Higher prices abroad may cut sales, so profits won't always rise."]}
{"id":"gbu-025","s":"business","t":"1.5.5","lv":2,"q":"Which is an example of a business responding to a change in technology?","o":["A café raises prices after the legal minimum wage goes up","A bank closes branches as more customers bank on their phones","A builder delays borrowing after interest rates rise","A shop cuts prices as unemployment rises in its area"],"a":1,"x":"External changes include technology, legislation and the economic climate. Customers moving to phone banking is a technology change. The minimum wage is legislation; interest rates and unemployment are the economic climate.","w":["Minimum wage is a law change (legislation), not a technology change.","","Interest rates are part of the economic climate, not technology.","Unemployment is the economic climate, not technology."]}
{"id":"gbu-026","s":"business","t":"2.1.1","lv":2,"q":"A private limited company becomes a plc. What can it now do?","o":["Avoid paying corporation tax on profit","Give its owners unlimited liability","Stop publishing its accounts each year","Sell shares to the public on the stock exchange"],"a":3,"x":"A plc can raise large sums by selling shares to the public. Its shareholders keep limited liability, and it must publish more information about itself.","w":["Plcs still pay corporation tax on profit; becoming a plc changes nothing here.","Wrong way round: shareholders in a plc keep limited liability.","Plcs must publish more information, not less, so this is the opposite.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-027","s":"business","t":"2.1.2","lv":2,"q":"A drinks firm sells mostly to teenagers. A new law bans selling its main drink to under-16s, so it changes its aim from growth to survival straight away. What caused this change?","o":["Technology","Internal reasons, such as a new owner","Legislation","Business performance"],"a":2,"x":"Aims can change because of market conditions, technology, performance, legislation or internal reasons. Here a new law forced the change, so the cause is legislation.","w":["No new technology is mentioned; the cause is the new law.","No internal change like a new owner is given; the law came from outside.","","Nothing suggests poor results; the ban was imposed from outside."]}
{"id":"gbu-028","s":"business","t":"2.1.3","lv":1,"q":"What is an import?","o":["A good or service bought from another country","A good or service sold to another country","A business with branches in many countries","A tax on goods coming into a country"],"a":0,"x":"Imports are bought from abroad; exports are sold abroad. A multinational operates in several countries, and a tax on imports is a tariff.","w":["","Mixed up with an export, which is sold abroad.","This describes a multinational, not an import.","This is a tariff, the tax on imports, not the import itself."]}
{"id":"gbu-029","s":"business","t":"2.1.4","lv":2,"q":"A firm pays suppliers fairly and avoids damaging the environment. Which is a likely benefit?","o":["Lower costs for the business in every case","Guaranteed higher profits every year","An end to competition from rivals","A better reputation that attracts customers"],"a":3,"x":"Ethical behaviour can improve reputation and customer loyalty. It may also raise costs, so higher profit is not guaranteed: there can be a trade-off between ethics and profit.","w":["Ethical choices often cost more, so costs are not always lower.","Profit is never guaranteed; ethics can raise costs and cut profit.","Rivals still compete; ethical behaviour does not remove competition.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-030","s":"business","t":"2.2.1","lv":2,"q":"A firm relaunches a falling product with a new flavour and packaging. What is this called?","o":["An extension strategy","Market research","A merger","Break-even"],"a":0,"x":"Extension strategies such as new packaging, new uses or price changes aim to lengthen the product's life cycle.","w":["","Market research finds out about customers; it is not relaunching a product.","A merger joins two firms, not a relaunch of one product.","Break-even is where revenue equals costs, not a relaunch."]}
{"id":"gbu-031","s":"business","t":"2.2.2","lv":2,"q":"A product costs £8 to make. The business adds a 40% mark-up using cost-plus pricing. What is the selling price?","o":["£8.40","£12.80","£11.20","£13.33"],"a":2,"x":"Mark-up = 40% of £8 = £3.20. Selling price = £8 + £3.20 = £11.20.","w":["Added 40p, not 40% of £8, which is £3.20.","Added 60% rather than 40%; mark-up is 40% of £8 = £3.20.","","Divided £8 by 0.6, treating 40% as a profit margin, not a mark-up on cost."]}
{"id":"gbu-032","s":"business","t":"2.2.3","lv":1,"q":"Which is a digital method of promotion?","o":["Printing a newspaper advert","Posting adverts on social media","Handing out leaflets","Putting up a poster"],"a":1,"x":"Digital promotion uses online channels such as social media, email and websites.","w":["A printed newspaper advert is traditional print media, not digital.","","Leaflets are printed, not online, so not digital.","Posters are printed, not online, so not digital."]}
{"id":"gbu-033","s":"business","t":"2.2.4","lv":1,"q":"What is an e-tailer?","o":["A shop with a high-street store","A retailer that sells goods online","A firm that makes goods","A firm that stores goods"],"a":1,"x":"E-tailers sell directly to customers over the internet. Retailers use shops.","w":["That is a high-street retailer; an e-tailer sells online.","","That describes a manufacturer, not a seller to the public.","That describes a warehouse or storage firm, not a seller."]}
{"id":"gbu-034","s":"business","t":"2.2.5","lv":3,"q":"A firm launches a premium watch. Each option gives product, price, promotion and place. Which marketing mix fits together best?","o":["High quality, low price, luxury magazine adverts, exclusive shops","High quality, high price, luxury magazine adverts, discount stores","High quality, high price, luxury magazine adverts, exclusive shops","High quality, high price, money-off coupons, exclusive shops"],"a":2,"x":"Each element of the mix should support the others. A premium watch needs a high price, upmarket promotion and exclusive outlets. A low price, coupons or discount stores would clash with its premium image.","w":["A low price clashes with a premium, high-quality image.","Discount stores clash with a premium image; place should be exclusive.","","Money-off coupons cheapen the image; promotion should be luxury."]}
{"id":"gbu-035","s":"business","t":"2.3.1","lv":1,"q":"Which type of production makes one unique item to a customer's order?","o":["Job production","Batch production","Flow production","Mass production"],"a":0,"x":"Job production makes one-off products, such as a bespoke wedding dress. Batch makes groups of identical items, flow makes continuously.","w":["","Batch makes groups of identical items, not one unique item.","Flow runs continuously for identical items, not one-offs.","Mass production makes lots of identical items, not a unique one."]}
{"id":"gbu-036","s":"business","t":"2.3.2","lv":2,"q":"A business uses just-in-time stock control. What is a benefit?","o":["No risk of late deliveries","Higher stock-holding costs","Bigger bulk-buying discounts","Lower storage costs"],"a":3,"x":"JIT means getting supplies only when needed, which cuts storage costs. It relies on reliable suppliers, and smaller, frequent orders usually mean losing bulk-buying discounts.","w":["JIT depends on suppliers, so late deliveries are a real risk.","JIT holds little stock, so stock-holding costs fall, not rise.","Small frequent orders usually lose bulk-buying discounts.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-037","s":"business","t":"2.3.3","lv":2,"q":"A bakery makes every worker check quality at each stage, to stop faults happening, rather than only inspecting the finished loaves. What is this approach?","o":["Quality control","Quality assurance","Just-in-time stock control","Flow production"],"a":1,"x":"Quality assurance checks quality at every stage to prevent faults. Quality control inspects finished products to find faults. Both help a business control costs and gain a competitive advantage.","w":["Mixes up the two: control inspects finished goods, this checks every stage.","","Just-in-time is about stock arriving when needed, not checking quality.","Flow production is a continuous production line, not a quality approach."]}
{"id":"gbu-038","s":"business","t":"2.3.4","lv":1,"q":"Which is an example of after-sales service?","o":["Offering free samples before purchase","Putting up a window display","Running an advert","Repairing a product after it is bought"],"a":3,"x":"After-sales service happens once the customer has bought, for example guarantees, repairs and returns help.","w":["Free samples come before purchase, so they are promotion, not after-sales.","Window displays attract customers before they buy, so not after-sales.","Adverts aim to win sales beforehand, so they are not after-sales service.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-039","s":"business","t":"2.4.1","lv":2,"q":"A business has revenue of £80,000 and cost of sales of £52,000. What is its gross profit margin?","o":["65%","54%","35%","28%"],"a":2,"x":"Gross profit = 80,000 - 52,000 = £28,000. Gross profit margin = 28,000 / 80,000 x 100 = 35%.","w":["Worked out cost of sales as a % of revenue (52 ÷ 80), not gross profit.","Divided gross profit by cost of sales (28 ÷ 52) instead of by revenue.","","Found the gross profit (£28,000) but never turned it into a % of revenue."]}
{"id":"gbu-040","s":"business","t":"2.4.1","lv":2,"q":"An investment of £40,000 earns total profit of £30,000 over 4 years. What is the average rate of return (ARR)?","o":["18.75%","75%","187.5%","7.5%"],"a":0,"x":"Average annual profit = 30,000 / 4 = £7,500. ARR = 7,500 / 40,000 x 100 = 18.75%.","w":["","Forgot to divide the total profit by 4 years to get the average yearly profit.","Place-value slip: 7,500 ÷ 40,000 × 100 gives 18.75%, not 187.5%.","Took the average profit (£7,500) as 7.5% without dividing by £40,000."]}
{"id":"gbu-041","s":"business","t":"2.4.1","lv":3,"q":"Project A costs £50,000 and earns £60,000 total profit over 5 years. Project B costs £30,000 and earns £42,000 total profit over 6 years. Which has the higher ARR?","o":["B, at 23.3%","A, at 120%","B, at 140%","A, at 24%"],"a":3,"x":"A: 60,000 / 5 = 12,000, which is 24% of 50,000. B: 42,000 / 6 = 7,000, which is 23.3% of 30,000. A is higher.","w":["Worked B out correctly, but 23.3% is lower than A's 24%.","Forgot to divide by 5 years: 60,000 ÷ 50,000 is the total return, not ARR.","Forgot to divide by 6 years, so B's ARR looks far too big.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-042","s":"business","t":"2.4.2","lv":3,"q":"Revenue rose from £100,000 to £120,000, but net profit fell from £10,000 to £9,000. What happened to the net profit margin?","o":["It fell from 10% to 7.5%","It rose from 10% to 12%","It stayed at 10%","It fell from 10% to 9%"],"a":0,"x":"Margin was 10,000 / 100,000 = 10%. Now it is 9,000 / 120,000 = 7.5%. Higher revenue does not mean higher margin.","w":["","Assumed higher revenue means a higher margin; profit actually fell.","Assumed the margin stays the same; 9,000 ÷ 120,000 is now only 7.5%.","Divided £9,000 by the old revenue (£100,000), not the new £120,000."]}
{"id":"gbu-043","s":"business","t":"2.5.1","lv":1,"q":"Compared with a hierarchical (tall) structure, a flat organisational structure has...","o":["More layers of management and a narrow span of control","Fewer layers of management and a wide span of control","Fewer layers of management and a narrow span of control","More layers of management and a wide span of control"],"a":1,"x":"A flat structure has few levels between top and bottom, so each manager oversees more staff (a wide span of control) and communication is quicker.","w":["That describes a tall structure: many layers and a narrow span of control.","","Mixed up span: fewer layers means each manager controls more staff, so wide.","Mixed up: more layers gives a narrow span, not a wide one."]}
{"id":"gbu-044","s":"business","t":"2.5.2","lv":1,"q":"Which recruitment document lists the tasks and responsibilities of a job?","o":["Curriculum vitae","Application form","Job description","Person specification"],"a":2,"x":"A job description sets out the tasks and duties of the role. A person specification describes the skills and qualities wanted; the CV and application form come from the applicant.","w":["A CV is written by the applicant to show their own history, not job tasks.","An application form is filled in by the applicant; it doesn't list job tasks.","","A person specification lists the skills and qualities wanted, not the tasks."]}
{"id":"gbu-045","s":"business","t":"2.5.3","lv":1,"q":"A new worker is shown how to use the tills by a colleague while serving customers. What is this?","o":["Formal training","A performance review","Self-learning","Informal training"],"a":3,"x":"Informal training happens while working, learning from an experienced colleague. Formal training is planned and structured, such as a course. Self-learning is when staff teach themselves.","w":["Formal training is planned and structured, like a course, not learning on the job.","A performance review assesses how someone is doing; it doesn't teach the tills.","Self-learning means teaching yourself; here a colleague shows the worker.",""]}
{"id":"gbu-046","s":"business","t":"2.5.4","lv":1,"q":"Which is a non-financial way to motivate staff?","o":["A bonus","Job rotation","Piece rate","Commission"],"a":1,"x":"Job rotation moves staff between tasks for variety. Bonuses, piece rate and commission are financial.","w":["A bonus is extra money, so it is a financial motivator.","","Piece rate pays per item made, so it is financial, not non-financial.","Commission is pay based on sales, so it is financial."]}
{"id":"gbu-047","s":"business","t":"2.5.4","lv":2,"q":"A worker is paid £6 per item (piece rate) plus a £20 bonus for quality. She makes 35 items. What does she earn?","o":["£210","£255","£230","£720"],"a":2,"x":"Piece rate pay = 35 x £6 = £210. Add the £20 bonus to get £230. Piece rate is a financial motivator linked to output.","w":["Worked out the piece rate (35 × £6) but left out the £20 bonus.","","",""]}
{"id":"gbu-048","s":"business","t":"2.4.2","lv":3,"q":"A firm's gross profit margin rose from 30% to 35%, but its net profit margin fell from 8% to 6%. What is the most likely reason?","o":["Overheads and expenses rose faster than gross profit","Cost of sales rose faster than revenue","Interest and other expenses fell","Selling prices were cut sharply"],"a":0,"x":"Gross margin rose, so cost of sales was controlled. Net margin falling means overheads such as rent and wages rose by more than the gain.","w":["","If cost of sales rose faster than revenue, gross margin would fall, but it rose.","Lower interest and expenses would raise net margin, not lower it.","Price cuts would squeeze gross margin, yet it rose from 30% to 35%."]}
